WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.410
Bismillah, alhamdulillah, salatu wa salam wa
ala rasulullah, hello calculus

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:08.810
students, today we're going to be taking
derivatives using the chain rule, as

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:13.950
we know this is the formal definition of the
chain rule, but I'll also show you the

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.630
power case which will be really helpful for
you guys, especially those that are

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:21.430
still in unit 1 of calculus and vectors, this
is really the only case you'll be

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.670
using the chain rule, let's get right into it. we
have some examples here that we're

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.200
going to go through. But first, I kind of want
to explain what both of those are

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.970
quickly. What this is saying right here, it
looks confusing, but all it's saying is

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.930
that what we want to do is when we have a
composite function, a composite function

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.370
is when we have a function inside
another function, how

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:46.350
we get its derivative is first by taking the
derivative of the outside, the outside

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:50.510
shell, and then taking the derivative of
whatever is inside and multiplying them

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.410
together okay Just like what we have here,
we're taking the derivative of the

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:58.310
outside with whatever's inside multiplied
by the derivative of the inside

00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:04.550
And the reason I mentioned the power rule
case in the introduction, and why I

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:09.650
think it is super useful for you guys right now
that's because since we have not yet

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.730
dived into any further calculus topics every
single chain rule question you guys are

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.210
going to be given is going to fall under the
power case anyways. So this will

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:25.190
always end up being the same question as this
until we start adding on more calculus

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.290
topics.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.670
Especially if you're in calculus and vectors
like I was when I was in high school,

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:36.290
you're not going to get any questions on the
exam or in your units or in your

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.070
homework other than the power case
for the chain rule itself.

00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.230
Either way, let's get started with the first
example. As we know, for the chain

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:49.510
rule, we have an f of x and a g
of x function. And we

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.610
should know how to extract both of them from
this equation. But here, we can tell

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:59.910
that our f of x function will be x to
the power of 5. And our

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.130
g of x function equals 2x
squared minus 3x.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:11.950
These two here, just to show you how we got them
from this we know that our g of x

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:17.430
is the inside function because it's
encapsulated by our power of five and then

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:21.970
that x to the power of five is that shell the
outside function like we have here in

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:27.130
our chain rule formula okay so here what
we want to do is take the

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.330
derivative, the f of x derivative,
which will give us using power

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:37.390
rule 5x to the 4, okay? And then
what we can now do is

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:42.750
plug in our g of x in place of where this x
is. Just like how when you're

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.270
doing first principles, and I showed you the
first principles example, it's the

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:50.410
exact same logic as when we were getting that f
of x plus h. What we do is we take

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:55.750
out this x, and we input the g of x. So
when we do that f prime of g of

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:56.030
x,

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:02.470
we're taking here, we would have 5x to
the 4 but we're taking this

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.470
x out and we're instead putting our g of x
function our g of x as written up here

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.810
is 2x cubed minus 3x and this will
all be to the power of 4.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:18.410
Okay the second thing we're going to need is
our g prime of x because we have to

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:22.530
multiply these two together to get our full
derivation of the chain rule. It's very

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:27.070
simple this one is just power rule again we have
2x squared oh sorry it's not cubed

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:32.510
here it is a squared 2x squared will be 4x and
negative 3x will just be negative 3

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:37.910
so now we have f prime of g of x and g
prime of x so we can just

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:43.170
multiply both of these and we will get
our final answer for our first

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:48.550
derivation so just y prime because that's
our full function is equal to

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.630
5 of 2x squared minus 3x to the power of 4

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.790
times 4x minus 3. On to our next
question. Here we have

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:05.220
h of x equals 1 over 5x minus 2 [to the

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:05.710
4].

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:12.110
Actually, very quickly before we move on to
this, this already follows the power

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:16.830
case to a t, right? Because if we look at
it here, what we have is we had

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:22.110
this whole function and we took the power
rule of it, we brought that

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:25.950
five in front and we subtract 1 [to get] the 4,
which matches exactly what the power

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.670
case would be, because our n here is five, and n
minus one is four, like our power.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.430
And then we took the prime, which is just what
was inside, which is this. So our

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:38.370
power case already matches exactly like I was
mentioning at the beginning, we're

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:42.190
going to do one more regular chain rule case,
and then we're just going to be doing

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:45.670
the power case. And I'll show you how it's
much, much simpler. Here, what we're

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:49.410
going to do is first, we want to rewrite because
we don't like our equation being in

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:52.810
the denominator so instead we're going to have it
as 5x minus 2 [to the negative 4],

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.530
and whenever we bring something up [to the]
numerator, we're going to have to do a

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.770
negative there switch the sign on
the power now from here

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:06.190
just like our first let's identify our f of x
and our g of x so our f of x here

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:11.610
will equal x to the negative 4 and
our g of x will equal 5x

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:16.970
minus 2. Again, g of x is our inside
function, and our f of x is that big

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:21.810
enclosed kind of like shell, like an onion shell
function, which is our negative 4.

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:27.310
So f prime of x will equal negative
4x to the negative 5

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.500
because we're going to bring that forward and
subtract 1. So that means [f] prime of

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:36.030
g of x. Remember, what we're doing is we're
taking that x out and plugging in our g

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:40.590
of x. It's going to be
negative 4, 5x minus 2

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:45.970
to the negative 5, okay? We can
also do our g prime of

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.800
x here, because we've got to multiply that
for the chain rule. g prime of

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:56.210
x equals 5 using power rule, and then
the negative 2 will go to 0,

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.320
so it just equals 5. Multiplying
these two together, we get h

00:06:01.170 --> 00:06:06.430
prime of x equals f prime of g of

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.930
x times g prime of x, which will be

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.940
negative 4 times 5 which is [negative]

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:21.130
20, 5x minus 2 to the power of

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:26.090
[negative] 5, or if you want to write it
in a fraction it would be

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:31.510
negative 20 over 5x minus 2

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:36.030
to the power of 5.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.520
and that is the answer for
chain rule in h of

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:46.830
x. For this example I just want to do the power
case because we don't even have to

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:51.250
worry about f of x at all when we're doing the
power case. Here, again, we have to

00:06:51.100 --> 00:06:55.610
do the same step first of bringing that up in
the numerator. So we're going to do

00:06:55.460 --> 00:07:01.010
that with the negative power on the 4. We're
going to switch the power on the 4. So

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:05.550
it's going to be 5x minus 2 to the power of
negative 4. But here we do not have to,

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:09.330
like I just mentioned, break it up into our f of
x and our g of x. We can just take

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:14.270
the power rule with respect to what's inside and
then multiply the derivative of the

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:17.850
inside, like our power case states. So here, what
we can do is we can just bring the

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:23.130
negative 4 forwards and then have 5x minus 2 and
then subtract 1. So it's negative

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:28.630
5, just like it says right over there. And
then we can multiply g prime of

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:33.170
x. And we already know g prime of x here is 5.
So all we have to do is multiply 5.

00:07:33.460 --> 00:07:38.950
And then we get, once again, negative
20, 5x minus 2 to the power

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.890
of negative 5. Or if you want as a
fraction, negative 20 over

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:47.410
5x minus 2 to the power of 5.

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:54.430
So we got the exact same answer in much fewer
steps. Yes, we already knew the g

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:58.630
prime of x, but even with that stuff included,
this is much, much quicker because we

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.110
don't have to worry about finding what our f of
x is and our g of x is because we

00:08:01.960 --> 00:08:05.850
know it's always going to be in the same form
because all we're doing is just having

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:10.870
a power to that g of x function. For our last
example here, we're going to solve the

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.200
derivative using the power case of the
chain rule. And we have to first,

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:19.430
as always, change the square root into a power
what we're going to do is we're going

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.650
to do x cubed plus 4x and we're going to have
this all to the power of 1 over 2

00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:25.270
because it's the same as a square root

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:31.870
and then instead of getting our f of x and our g
of x we can just apply that power

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:36.230
case we can just take the power rule with
respect to what's inside that's the same

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:40.970
as doing 1 over 2, x cubed plus 4x, and we're
going to subtract 1 from that so

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:44.230
that's going to become negative 1 over 2 and
we're going to multiply the derivative

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:48.990
of the inside that is 3x
squared plus 4 using

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:54.130
the power rule again this will become 3x

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:59.470
squared plus 4 over 2 times the square

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:04.730
root of x cubed plus 4x if you want
to instead put it in

00:09:04.580 --> 00:09:10.230
the fraction and square root
form and that is the

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:13.470
examples of the chain rule and power case
